The servicing app's payoff engine, ported for the payoff-statement
environment's grader.

Source       apps/servicing/src/domain/payoff/{quote,remit}.ts
             apps/servicing/src/domain/investor/{remittance,types}.ts
Vendored at  293261b371fe97d2b6b39e1f29ca60dd808ac8fb (apps/servicing/VENDORED_FROM)
Ported       2026-10-21

Each file here is its source byte for byte except its import lines: the
kernel's paths become @hm/kernel/{money,calendar} (the same bytes;
packages/kernel/VENDORED_FROM), a sibling's .ts becomes .js, and remit.ts's
"../investor/remittance.ts" becomes "./remittance.js", because the two
investor files sit beside the payoff ones here. Nothing else may be edited:
src/__tests__/payoff.test.ts compares each pair with their import lines
removed, holds each import line to that rewrite, and fails on any
difference. Lint and Prettier skip this directory for the reason they skip
the vendored trees.

remittance.ts comes because remit.ts takes Fannie Mae's payoff interest from
it (5.2 rule 6, payoffInterest); types.ts because remittance.ts imports its
RemittanceType (a type only; the file's runtime constants are never used
here). remit.ts's aaRemittanceClock reads time zones through the kernel's
zoned.ts; v1 never calls it. release.ts (16.3, lien release) is not ported:
v1 does not grade lien release.

What the engine takes from its caller (the payoff date under F-1-09, the
exact figure as of it, the reliance flags, the S/S BD1/BD2 exception and the
day it is processed) lives in the servicing app's stateful ops layer
(ops-16-2.ts) and its runtime tools, which do not port; it is restated in
src/payoff/grader.ts with the lines it comes from, and the test holds those
lines to the source.

To take a change from the servicing app, copy the four files again and
rewrite the same imports.
